[PATCH] sf2000: SF2000 volume to 30 (0x1e) for the low-gain experiment

Physical-device runs 370-372 showed no quality change between volume
bytes 0xff/0xcb/0x61, so analog overdrive is probably not the crackle
source.  Lower the SF2000 gain to volume 30 on the vendor curve (0x1e)
for the requested experiment; the unit has a hardware volume rocker so
the digital gain can stay low.  GB300 stays at UniFrog value 75 (0x8f).

Signed-off-by: SF2000 Linux project <noreply@example.com>
---
diff --git a/sound/drivers/sf2000-pcm.c b/sound/drivers/sf2000-pcm.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
@@ -66,15 +66,16 @@
 #define SF2000_I2S_DIV_CTL_BITS	(BIT(16) | BIT(21))
 /*
  * Default SND0+0x92 FADE volume bytes.  The vendor libauddrv maps a 0-100
- * volume through a non-linear table onto this byte (100 -> 0xff); the stock
- * SF2000 device tree ships the i2so platform at volume 90, which lands at
- * 0xcb on that curve - the same byte the bootloader uses.  UniFrog lowers
- * it to 65 (0x61) on SF2000 and 75 (0x8f) on GB300 at runtime.  The raw
- * maximum (0xff) with full-scale game samples overdrives the small analog
- * speaker: audible clipping, harsh treble and crackle; match the stock
- * default instead.
+ * volume through a non-linear table onto this byte (100 -> 0xff, 90 -> 0xcb,
+ * 65 -> 0x61, 30 -> 0x1e).  The stock SF2000 device tree ships the i2so
+ * platform at volume 90; the bootloader writes 0xcb and UniFrog lowers to
+ * 65 (SF2000) / 75 (GB300).  The SF2000 value here is the low-gain
+ * experiment requested on the physical unit (volume 30 -> 0x1e) to test
+ * whether the remaining crackle is analog overdrive; the unit has a
+ * hardware volume rocker so the digital gain can stay low.  GB300 stays at
+ * UniFrog's 0x8f.
  */
-#define SF2000_VOLUME_SF2000	0xcb
+#define SF2000_VOLUME_SF2000	0x1e
 #define SF2000_VOLUME_GB300	0x8f
 #define SF2000_RATE_MIN		8000
 #define SF2000_RATE_MAX		48000
